#usda 1.0
(
    doc = """The whole character, one layer per thing it is made of.

    Open this and the Layer Stack view reads top to bottom as the order
    opinions are resolved, strongest first. Every layer here contributes
    one kind of data and composes over the ones below it, so any of them
    can be republished without touching the rest, and any of them can be
    dropped to get a lighter character:

      Biped_picker.usda         the control picker: RigExecPicker prims,
                                found by type. Strongest, because it is
                                the layer an animator overrides -- a
                                button moved or deactivated wins over
                                the published layout.

      Biped_touch_regions.usda  TouchPose regions. `over`s only: it
                                annotates the mesh with named face sets
                                and the control each drives, and adds no
                                geometry of its own, so it has to sit
                                above what it annotates.

      Biped_psd.usda            the pose-space deformation: one
                                RigExecPoseInterpolator per corrective,
                                each reading joint poses and publishing
                                a weight.

      Biped_face_rig.usda       the face, as its own branch of the
                                tree: the face layer and the eyes,
                                mouth, brows and cheeks over it, each
                                still its own layer (see that file).

      Biped_shapes.usdc         the blend shapes those weights drive --
                                UsdSkelBlendShape targets and the mover
                                that applies them. Crate, because it is
                                four megabytes of point offsets.

      Biped_layered.usda        the rig, which is itself four layers
                                (see below).

    The middle two are deliberately useless apart: the interpolators
    publish weights nothing reads, and the shapes wait for weights nobody
    writes. Together they are the corrective system, and either one alone
    is inert rather than broken, which is the property that lets a shot
    drop the correctives without editing anything.

    Biped_layered.usda brings the rig in as four more layers:

      Biped_layered_left.usda   the left limbs
      Biped_layered_right.usda  the right limbs
      Biped_layered_center.usda the spine, neck, head and the rig root
      Biped_layered_model.usda  the geometry and materials

    Left, right and centre are rig layers stacked on the model layer, so
    the model is publishable on its own and the rig is three files that
    two riggers can work in at once without colliding."""
    defaultPrim = "Biped"
    metersPerUnit = 0.01
    upAxis = "Y"
    subLayers = [
        @./Biped_picker.usda@,
        @./Biped_touch_regions.usda@,
        @./Biped_psd.usda@,
        @./Biped_face_rig.usda@,
        @./Biped_shapes.usdc@,
        @./Biped_layered.usda@
    ]
)

# VIEWING DEFAULTS, and only viewing defaults. This is the layer that
# assembles the character, so it is where an opinion about what is drawn
# on open belongs -- not in the rig layers, which should describe the rig
# the same way whoever opens them wants to see it.
#
# Joints and solvers are hidden. They are the rig's internals: an
# animator grabs CONTROLS, and 252 joint guides plus the solver guides
# on top of them bury the controls they are meant to sit behind. Both
# come back with a `visibility = "inherited"` opinion in any stronger
# layer, or by unchecking them in the viewer, so nothing is lost -- the
# rig still publishes every one of them.
over "Biped"
{
    over "Rig"
    {
        over "Joints"
        {
            token visibility = "invisible"
        }

        over "Solvers"
        {
            token visibility = "invisible"
        }
    }
}
